**Theorem 11.1 (concrete specimen and exact scalar relaxation).** Let

$$
X=\sqrt{r_S},\quad Y=\sqrt{r_S/r_T},\quad Z=\sqrt2,\quad
N=X^2+Y^2+Z^2,\qquad \psi=(X,iY,Z)^T/\sqrt N .
\tag{PG.59}
$$

Its physical Gram has rank two and

$$
C_{DT}=-i\chi_TXY/N,\qquad
C_{DS}=\kappa_{02}XZ/N>0,\qquad
C_{TS}=-iacYZ/N .
\tag{PG.60}
$$

Its unique kernel is generated by
$n=(1,-ih_1Y/(bX),-h_2Z/(dX))^T$, with $n^TBn=0$ but $n^\dagger Bn<0$.

For this $C$, the scalar-trace relaxation
$\min\{\operatorname{tr}(CH):0\preceq H\preceq I,\operatorname{tr}(BH)\ge k\}$
has the following exact unique solution. Take the positive generalized root $\lambda_0$ of (PG.25), its normalized kernel $v_0$ with positive $D$ coordinate, and $b_0=v_0^\dagger Bv_0>0$. If $b_0\ge k$, put

$$
\lambda=\lambda_0,\quad \mu=0,\quad
v=v_0,\quad a=k/b_0,\quad H_{\rm rel}=a vv^\dagger .
$$

If $b_0<k$, take the unique $\lambda>\lambda_0$ on the simple top eigenbranch with $v^\dagger Bv=k$, and put
$\mu=\lambda_{\max}(\lambda B-C)>0$, $a=1$, $H_{\rm rel}=vv^\dagger$.
In either case

$$
F_{\rm rel}=k\lambda-\mu>0,\qquad
(H_{\rm rel})_{DT}\ \hbox{is nonzero purely imaginary}.
\tag{PG.61}
$$

Proof. The specimen identity is
$X^2=r_T(iY)^2+r_SZ^2=-r_S+2r_S$.
Theorem 4.2 and direct substitution in (PG.19) give (PG.60) and its kernel. All three displayed off-diagonal entries are nonzero. The nonzero imaginary $T$ ratio makes its Hermitian $B$ value strictly negative by the triangle equality characterization.

The lower block $C_N$ is positive definite; otherwise extending its kernel by zero $D$ coordinate would give a second Gram kernel. Set $A_N=\operatorname{diag}(\alpha,\beta)$,
$a(\lambda)=(C_N+\lambda A_N)^{-1}c_{ND}$ and
$f(\lambda)=c_{DD}-\lambda-c_{DN}(C_N+\lambda A_N)^{-1}c_{ND}$.
Then

$$
f(0)=0,\quad f'(0)=-1+a(0)^\dagger A_Na(0)>0,\quad
f'=-1+a^\dagger A_Na,\quad
f''=-2a^\dagger A_N(C_N+\lambda A_N)^{-1}A_Na<0 .
$$

The first strict sign is exactly the negative Hermitian kernel value, since its normalization with first coordinate one is $(1,-a(0))$. Coupling is nonzero, so the second strict sign holds at every nonnegative $\lambda$. Also $f(\lambda)\to-\infty$. Thus it has a unique positive zero, the root (PG.25), and $f'(\lambda_0)<0$. Its Schur complement proves $C-\lambda_0B\succeq0$ of rank two. Its normalized kernel has
$b_0=-f'(\lambda_0)/\|(1,-a(\lambda_0))\|^2>0$.

For $\lambda\ge\lambda_0$, the simple top eigenvalue starts at zero. Its Hermitian derivative formula is
$\mu'=b=v^\dagger Bv$ and
$b'=2\sum_{j\ne{\rm top}}|v_j^\dagger Bv|^2/(\mu-\mu_j)>0$.
The proof is the projected simple-eigenpair differentiation of (PG.29). If the derivative vanished, positive $b$ would force $v=e_D$, contrary to its nonzero couplings. The same $e_D$ Rayleigh bound gives $b\to1$. This proves the two spectral cases and the unique crossing. In either case
$S_{\rm rel}=C-\lambda B+\mu vv^\dagger$ is positive of rank two with kernel $v$. The identity

$$
\operatorname{tr}(CH)-F_{\rm rel}
=\lambda[\operatorname{tr}(BH)-k]+\operatorname{tr}(S_{\rm rel}H)
 +\mu[1-v^\dagger Hv]
\tag{PG.62}
$$

proves optimality and uniqueness. If $\mu=0$, it forces $H=a vv^\dagger$ and $a=k/b_0$; if $\mu>0$, it forces $H=vv^\dagger$. Positivity of $F_{\rm rel}$ follows because a positive-$B$ vector cannot lie on the nonpositive-$B$ Gram kernel.

To inspect this eigenpair only, use $G=\operatorname{diag}(1,i,1)$ and $C'=G^\dagger CG$. It is real symmetric, with $C'_{DT},C'_{DS}>0$ and $C'_{TS}<0$. The selected $v'=G^\dagger v$ can be real with $v'_D>0$. If $v'_T=0$, its $T$ equation
$C'_{TD}v'_D+C'_{TS}v'_S=0$ forces $v'_S>0$. Its $S$ equation is then
$C'_{SD}v'_D+(C'_{SS}+\beta\lambda+\mu)v'_S=0$, impossible. Hence $v'_T\ne0$, and
$(H_{\rm rel})_{DT}=-ia v'_Dv'_T\ne0$. This change of coordinates is used only for spectral inspection; it is not asserted to preserve the bilinear singular locus or the true low cone. $\square$

